CodeGuard
=========

Scanner rules shipped with CodeGuard come from two places. They are kept in
separate directories and never mixed.

Third-party rules (vendored, unmodified)
----------------------------------------

rules_vendor/gitlab-sast-rules/
  GitLab sast-rules v2.10.1: JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, Java and C# rules.
  MIT (c) GitLab Inc.; the Go rules derived from gosec are Apache-2.0.
  Source, archive checksum, what was and was not taken, and the full license
  text: rules_vendor/gitlab-sast-rules/NOTICE and LICENSE.

CodeGuard's own rules
---------------------

rules/
  The Python AI-aware ruleset (rules/llm-security.yaml).

rules_lang/
  Rules CodeGuard wrote for the non-Python languages, run alongside the
  vendored pack. Not derived from it. Every rule id starts with
  "codeguard-", and their findings are tagged "codeguard-rules", never
  "gitlab-sast".
  * go-insecure-skip-verify.yaml (codeguard-go-tls-insecure-skip-verify):
    tls.Config with InsecureSkipVerify set to true, outside *_test.go files.
  * go-math-rand-secret.yaml (codeguard-go-math-rand-secret):
    math/rand used for a security-sensitive value (token, key, secret,
    password, nonce, salt, session, uuid, invite/reset id).
  * java-runtime-exec-injection.yaml (codeguard-java-runtime-exec-injection):
    a request value or String parameter reaching Runtime.getRuntime().exec.
  * dockerfile-security.yaml (codeguard-dockerfile-*): nine Dockerfile
    checks -- a :latest base image, curl/wget piped into a shell, ADD from a
    URL with no checksum, certificate or signature checks turned off, a
    credential in ENV/ARG, a credential passed as a build argument,
    chmod 777, an SSH private key copied in, and a final USER root.
  * js-llm-security.yaml (codeguard-js-llm-*): the Python AI-aware ruleset
    ported to the OpenAI and Anthropic JavaScript/TypeScript SDKs -- prompt
    injection (including through a variable), model output into
    eval/new Function/vm/a shell or a SQL query, a hardcoded API key, no
    output cap, no client timeout, a "-latest" model alias, logging the
    full response, and no system/user separation.

Third-party scanners (run, not vendored)
----------------------------------------

zizmor 1.30.1 (MIT), https://github.com/zizmorcore/zizmor
  GitHub Actions workflows. Installed in the image by hash
  (Dockerfile, /opt/zizmor), run offline, never given a GitHub token.

OSV-Scanner 2.6.0 (Apache-2.0), https://github.com/google/osv-scanner
  Known vulnerabilities in npm/yarn/pnpm, Go, Maven/Gradle and NuGet
  lockfiles. Installed in the image by hash (Dockerfile, /opt/osv-scanner).
  Queries OSV.dev for vulnerability data; never resolves or builds
  anything, and is given no credential.
